meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
=========================

Graphics-stack-independent fast-boot work for the RZ/G3L SMARC EVK.

  Phase 0   U-Boot environment: no boot delay, no environment reset, quiet
            kernel command line.
  Phase 1   Device-tree pruning and kernel configuration trimming.
  Phase 2   Rootfs slimming and systemd startup-order optimisation.
  Phase 2b  Removal of the remaining blocks on the boot critical path, each
            located with systemd-analyze rather than guessed at.

It keeps the BSP's Weston graphics stack. Build core-image-weston with the
rz-vlp-fast distro to reproduce the baseline.

docs/fast-boot-method.md generalises the method behind phase 2b - the
measurement discipline, the cost taxonomy and the three patterns - for reuse on
other boards.


I. What this layer changes
==========================

  U-Boot (recipes-bsp/u-boot/)
    fastboot.cfg sets CONFIG_BOOTDELAY=0. The patch drops "env default -a" from
    bootcmd, which otherwise reset the whole environment on every boot, and
    rewrites prodemmcbootargs to a quiet command line without earlycon.
    prodsdbootargs keeps the BSP default, so only the eMMC product path goes
    quiet. The bbappend then asserts the compiled-in defaults really contain
    those values (section IV).

  Kernel (recipes-kernel/linux/)
    The DT patch collects the unused nodes - network, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera,
    audio, extra UARTs, RTC - into rzg3l-smarc-fastboot.dtsi and includes it at
    the end of the board .dts. fastboot.cfg removes the matching kernel
    subsystems, drops CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS and the wireless subsystem (VI.1), and
    asserts positively that the display, boot storage and thermal paths survive.

  Distro (conf/distro/rz-vlp-fast.conf)
    Derives from rz-vlp, pins linux-renesas 6.12, removes unused
    DISTRO_FEATURES / MACHINE_FEATURES and the RZ/VLP development and benchmark
    package groups, disables autostart for the corresponding services, and
    fixes SERIAL_CONSOLES to ttySC3.

  Userspace
    fastboot-systemd-tweaks.bbclass masks vconsole setup, the invalid ttyS0
    getty and systemd's network/identity services, and credits the saved random
    seed so sysinit.target stops waiting for the CRNG (VI.2).
    systemd-conf_%.bbappend keeps journald in RAM and stops serial forwarding.
    weston-init.bbappend lets Weston start in parallel with multi-user.target.


II. Required layers
===================

A standard RZ/G3L VLP project:

  poky/meta
  poky/meta-poky
  poky/meta-yocto-bsp
  meta-openembedded/meta-oe
  meta-openembedded/meta-python
  meta-openembedded/meta-multimedia
  meta-arm/meta-arm-toolchain
  meta-arm/meta-arm
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-bsp
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-distro
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-codecs
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-graphics
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

This layer has priority 20 and must follow the Renesas BSP, distro and graphics
layers in bblayers.conf.

Tested with Yocto scarthgap, RZ/G3L BSP v1.0.0, u-boot 2024.07 and
linux-renesas 6.12.46-cip8, MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l".

  Stacking with meta-rzg3l-fast-logo
  ----------------------------------
  The two layers are independent and can be used in either order or on their
  own. This one never touches the display path; that one never touches anything
  else. Both patch r9a08g046l48-smarc.dts, but in disjoint places, and both are
  cut against the BSP version above.

  Put meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base first in bblayers.conf. Both layers are
  priority 20, so the order in which their kernel patches are applied follows
  bblayers.conf, and that is the order the combination was tested in.


III. Build
==========

  $ source poky/oe-init-build-env build
  $ bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

Set in conf/local.conf:

  MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l"
  DISTRO  = "rz-vlp-fast"

Then:

  $ bitbake core-image-weston

Note that adding the layer alone already applies phases 0 and 1 - they come
from bbappends. Phases 2 and 2b need DISTRO = "rz-vlp-fast", which is what
pulls in fastboot-systemd-tweaks.

Metadata checks:

  $ bitbake-layers show-layers
  $ bitbake-layers show-appends
  $ bitbake -e core-image-weston | \
        grep -E '^(DISTRO|PREFERRED_VERSION_linux-renesas|SERIAL_CONSOLES)='
  $ grep -E '^(bootdelay|bootcmd|prod.*bootargs)=' \
        tmp/deploy/images/smarc-rzg3l/u-boot-initial-env-smarc-rzg3l


IV. U-Boot environment
======================

Phase 0 is compiled into the bootloader, so there is nothing to type on a
freshly flashed board. `bitbake u-boot` produces:

  bootdelay=0
  bootcmd=run bootcmd_check;run bootimage
  prodemmcbootargs=setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 quiet systemd.show_status=false

Against the stock BSP that is three changes:

  bootdelay          2 -> 0. A keypress on the serial console still interrupts
                     autoboot.

  bootcmd            loses the leading "env default -a", which reset the whole
                     environment one command before using it - discarding any
                     saved tuning and making the environment read-only in
                     practice.

  prodemmcbootargs   loses "earlycon" and gains "quiet
                     systemd.show_status=false". earlycon replays the ring
                     buffer synchronously over the 115200-baud UART and keeps
                     every later printk at that speed. Nothing is lost from the
                     kernel log or the journal, only from the serial output.
                     Re-add earlycon when debugging a failure that happens
                     before the real console is registered.

These live in the bootloader, not the rootfs, so the board only picks them up
once the newly built fip has been written to it. Reflashing only the rootfs
leaves the old bootloader and the old two-second delay.

  Boards that already have a saved environment
  --------------------------------------------
  The values above are the compiled-in *defaults*, used only while the
  environment area is empty or fails its CRC. A board that has had `saveenv`
  run on it keeps its stored copy. Switch it over once, after flashing:

    => env default -a
    => saveenv
    => reset

  Verify with `env print bootdelay bootcmd prodemmcbootargs`.

Either mechanism can fail silently on a BSP update, so the bbappend re-reads
u-boot-initial-env after do_compile and calls bbfatal if any of the three
entries is missing.


V. Product constraints
======================

The DT profile disables Ethernet, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio, i2c0/i2c1/
i2c3, extra UARTs and RTC. Restore both the DT node and its matching kernel and
userspace features before adding any of those product functions.

i2c2 stays enabled - it carries the ADV7535 HDMI bridge. adc1 stays enabled -
it is the TSU thermal-protection input. The microSD controller stays enabled
for the development boot loop; a fixed eMMC product can disable it once
manufacturing and recovery requirements are settled.

systemd's resolved, networkd, timesyncd and userdbd are masked. Adding any
network function back means un-masking them together with the DT node and the
DISTRO_FEATURES entry.


VI. Phase 2b: the boot critical path
====================================

Phase 2 left userspace at roughly 75% of the boot. `systemd-analyze plot`
showed why: the path to graphical.target is not a broad parallel fan-out, it is
six strictly serial segments. Because they are serial, the savings add.

Baseline (RZ/G3L EVK, phase 2 + meta-rzg3l-fast-logo, eMMC boot):

  553 ms kernel + 6.284 s userspace = 6.837 s

  segment                                    kernel-rel     cost
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  kernel -> systemd runnable                 0     .. 0.553   553 ms
  systemd start-up + sysinit.target          0.553 .. 2.757  2204 ms   VI.2
  basic.target / sockets / user-sessions     2.757 .. 2.883   126 ms
  512 legacy PTY device units                2.883 .. 3.997  1114 ms   VI.1
  getty.target / multi-user.target           3.997 .. 4.069    72 ms
  weston.service                             4.069 .. 6.727  2658 ms   VI.3
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  graphical.target                                   6.729

VI.1 and VI.2 remove one segment each, roughly -1.1 s and -0.6 s, so
graphical.target should land near 5.0 s. Those are projections from the
baseline plot, not measured results - re-measure per VI.4 and correct this
table.

`systemd-analyze plot` uses a kernel-relative x axis; `critical-chain` prints
userspace-relative `@` times. The two differ by exactly the 553 ms kernel
phase. All figures here are kernel-relative, so they line up with dmesg.

Do not use the serial `login:` prompt as the metric. serial-getty is Type=idle,
so it deliberately waits for the job queue to drain - in the baseline it prints
at 7.897 s, 1.2 s after the boot has actually finished.


VI.1 512 legacy PTY device units - 1114 ms
------------------------------------------

CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS creates 256 pty pairs. udev emits an event per device and
systemd instantiates two device units for each, so the boot transaction carries
512 units that nothing will ever open - 81% of every unit in the plot. systemd
walks them serially, and that walk lands between systemd-user-sessions
finishing (2.980 s) and weston being allowed to start (4.069 s). The same
events are also most of the work in systemd-udev-trigger.service (1.049 s) and
the reason dev-mmcblk0p2.device does not settle until 2.757 s.

Fixed in fastboot.cfg. CONFIG_UNIX98_PTYS is asserted positively in the same
fragment so a defconfig change can never leave the board with no pty support.

The Image does not get smaller - legacy ptys are a few KB of code. The win is
unit count, not size.

The wireless subsystem goes at the same time. CONFIG_WLAN has to be the symbol
disabled: drivers/net/wireless/Kconfig does "select WIRELESS" and WLAN is
default y, so turning off WIRELESS alone is silently reverted by kconfig.
Always verify a negative fragment entry against the generated .config (VI.4).


VI.2 sysinit.target - 2204 ms
-----------------------------

`critical-chain` names systemd-resolved as the last link, but the plot shows
four services finishing within 110 ms of each other, all gating the same
target:

  systemd-timesyncd      1.886 .. 2.556   669 ms
  systemd-random-seed    1.623 .. 2.622   998 ms   <- crng init done @2.590
  systemd-resolved       1.873 .. 2.640   766 ms   <- on the critical chain
  systemd-networkd       1.926 .. 2.665   739 ms
  sysinit.target                 2.757

Both causes have to go in the same step; removing either one alone just
promotes the other to be the new gate.

  Network and identity services. The DT profile has no Ethernet, so networkd,
  resolved, timesyncd, userdbd and network-generator have nothing to do. They
  ship inside the systemd recipe, so SYSTEMD_AUTO_ENABLE cannot reach them;
  they are masked in the rootfs instead. See FASTBOOT_MASKED_UNITS.

  Entropy. systemd-random-seed is blocked, not slow: after writing the saved
  seed it generates a fresh one, and that blocks in getrandom() until the CRNG
  initialises. Crediting the seed initialises it on the spot. The bbclass
  records why crediting is safe here and what the first-boot caveat is.

VI.1 helps here too: dev-mmcblk0p2.device settles at 2.757 s, exactly when
sysinit.target completes, because udev's queue is clogged with the pty events.


VI.3 weston.service - 2658 ms (withdrawn)
-----------------------------------------

Stock weston.service runs the compositor as User=weston with
PAMName=weston-autologin. pam_systemd asks logind for a session and logind
brings up a complete per-user service manager for uid 998:

  4.069s  systemd forks the weston executor, PAM starts
  6.153s  user@998.service ready
  6.727s  weston active -> graphical.target

2118 ms of the 2658 ms is session setup; the compositor itself needs 540 ms.

weston-init.bbappend therefore used to drop User=, Group=, PAMName= and the
systemd-user-sessions dependency.

*** WITHDRAWN: this deadlocks suspend to RAM. ***

Without a logind session weston falls back to its launcher-direct backend and
takes the VT over itself - KDSETMODE(KD_GRAPHICS) and VT_SETMODE(VT_PROCESS) on
the tty systemd hands it through TTYPath=/dev/tty7. It then becomes the process
change_console() must hand-shake with before any VT switch can complete, and it
never answers. pm_prepare_console() moves the console to SUSPEND_CONSOLE before
a single task is frozen, so "echo mem > /sys/power/state" never reaches the
freezer:

  __vt_event_wait <- vt_waitactive <- vt_move_to_console
                  <- pm_prepare_console <- pm_suspend <- state_store

On the bench: compositor still on screen, AWO and ALL_ON lit with DDR_RET dark,
SLEEP_BTN dead because ATF is never entered, and only a power cycle recovers.

So the hunk is gone and weston runs in its logind session again, which is the
configuration S2R is known to work in on this board. The bbappend now asserts
the opposite of what it used to: bbfatal if User=, Group= or PAMName= is
missing.

Two alternatives were considered and rejected:

  - Re-enable DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION so register_framebuffer() casts the
    pm_vt_switch_required(dev, false) vote. It fixes the deadlock but destroys
    the meta-rzg3l-fast-logo boot logo: drm_fb_helper_hotplug_event() calls
    set_par() on every connector hotplug event, committing the all-black fbdev
    framebuffer over the picture. Tried on hardware; the logo lasted a few
    hundred milliseconds. It also only works while console suspend is enabled.

  - Lock VT switching with VT_LOCKSWITCH. This keeps the 2.1 s but needs a new
    helper binary and races weston's own VT_ACTIVATE at start-up. Viable, but
    not without hardware time.

Getting the 2.1 s back means keeping the session and stopping weston from
having to arbitrate VT switches. It does not mean re-enabling fbdev emulation.


VI.4 Re-measuring
-----------------

Check the kernel side on the build host first - a negative fragment entry can
be silently reverted by a "select", or name a symbol that no longer exists:

  $ bitbake -c kernel_configcheck -f linux-renesas
  $ C=$(find tmp/work/*/linux-renesas/*/linux-*-build -name .config)
  $ grep -E '^(CONFIG_X=|# CONFIG_X is not set)' $C   # for each X in fastboot.cfg

Then on the target:

  systemd-analyze
  systemd-analyze blame
  systemd-analyze critical-chain graphical.target
  systemd-analyze plot > /boot.svg

Read the numbers from `systemd-analyze`, not from the serial log. Take ten
boots and record the P95: systemd-random-seed blocks for a variable time, so a
single best-case boot is not representative.

When measuring anything that logs at info level, remember that
systemd-conf_%.bbappend sets MaxLevelStore=warning - raise it to info for the
measurement boot or the records will not be in the journal.
